From: Joan Karadimov <joan.karadimov@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Ask whether there is a database, not whether Arch's is there

hasPacmanDatabase() looks for /var/lib/pacman/sync/core.db, or system.db on
Artix. Naming a repository is a proxy for the question, and the proxy only
holds on a distribution that has that repository. MSYS2's are msys, ucrt64,
mingw64 and the rest, so core.db is never there, and octopi opens with
"Pacman Database is missing!" over an empty package list.

On Windows it asks the question instead: is there any .db in the sync
directory. The path goes through msysPath() for the same reason as the rest.

Not for upstream as-is, though the generic form would be the better check
everywhere.
diff --git a/src/package.cpp b/src/package.cpp
index 95367af..7d4c12c 100644
--- a/src/package.cpp
+++ b/src/package.cpp
@@ -33,6 +33,7 @@
 #include <QTextStream>
 #include <QList>
 #include <QFile>
+#include <QDir>
 #include <QTemporaryFile>
 #include <QRegularExpression>
 #include <QEventLoop>
@@ -2531,6 +2532,19 @@ bool Package::hasPacmanDatabase()
 
   if (!done)
   {
+#ifdef Q_OS_WIN
+    // Naming a repository is a proxy for the real question, and the proxy
+    // only holds on a distribution that has that repository. MSYS2's are
+    // msys, ucrt64, mingw64 and the rest, so core.db is never there and the
+    // answer would always be no. Ask the question itself instead.
+    {
+      QDir sync(UnixCommand::msysPath(ctn_PACMAN_DATABASE_DIR)
+                + QLatin1String("/sync"));
+      answer = !sync.entryList(QStringList() << QStringLiteral("*.db"),
+                               QDir::Files).isEmpty();
+      done = true;
+    }
+#else
     if (UnixCommand::getLinuxDistro() == ectn_ARTIXLINUX)
     {
       QFile f(ctn_PACMAN_SYSTEM_DB_FILE);
@@ -2543,6 +2557,7 @@ bool Package::hasPacmanDatabase()
       answer = f.exists();
       done = true;
     }
+#endif
   }
 
   return answer;
